task: dataflow-exchange
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/dataflow-exchange
worktree: ../okay-wt-dfx
spec: specs/dataflow.md, stage 2

why: stage 1 finishes a keyed stage by merging every partition's
  partials at ONE coordinator. That is the right plan when the merged
  result fits one node and the wrong one when it does not, and the
  spec's Claim 1 says the plan should be able to tell. Today there is
  no other road to pick, so the claim is untested.

what: `Finish` on the keyed and windowed nodes — Merge (today),
  Shuffle(reducers), Auto — a hash exchange above the map-side
  combine, and the rule about `Sequential` made precise in both
  directions: a Sequential KEYED aggregator stays safe under a
  shuffle (each owner still merges its share by partition index), a
  Sequential TERMINAL aggregator is refused when there is more than
  one reducer, because the reducers' outputs are in no input order at
  all.

  And the crossover MEASURED, framed honestly: in one process the
  shuffle saves no memory — every partial is already here. What it
  buys is that the final merge runs on R threads instead of one. So
  the number to find is the accumulator count at which parallelising
  that merge beats the handoff. The memory argument is stage 4's and
  must not be claimed here.

predict: Wrocław is ~8 x 3300 = 2.6e4 merges, which I expect to be
  far BELOW the crossover — i.e. the shuffle should LOSE on the
  benchmark this engine exists for, and the honest outcome of this
  lane may well be "the exchange is the road you take when you have
  to, and here is the number that says when". MEASURE it rather than
  write that sentence in advance.
